# Line endings are part of the evidence here.
#
# gamedev-evidence.json hashes 44 source files by their bytes ON DISK, and
# the course checker recomputes those hashes from a clone. Git's autocrlf is
# on by default on Windows, so a fresh clone there rewrote every LF to CRLF
# and the checker failed on the first file it looked at:
#
#     Godot gamedev evidence FAIL: Source hash mismatch:
#     assets/materials/hint_with_source_color.tres
#
# 982 bytes in the repository, 1004 in the clone - twenty-two carriage
# returns that nobody typed. Found on 2026-10-28 by cloning the a5 tag into
# an empty directory and running the checker against it, which is the only
# way it shows up: the machine the film was built on has the right bytes.
#
# So: no conversion, anywhere. What is committed is what lands on disk.
* -text

# One exception. cmd.exe is happier with CRLF in a batch file, and nothing
# hashes this one.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Seven files the ledger hashes are CRLF in the author's working tree and LF
# in the repository: they were typed in an editor that writes CRLF, and
# autocrlf normalised them on the way in. The hashes were taken over the
# bytes on disk, so a clone has to reproduce the CRLF or the checker fails -
# which is what `* -text` alone did, on features/traps/spike_trap.gd.
#
# These lines change no committed blob and no file on disk. They only say
# what checkout should write, and `godot/shaders/*` in particular keeps the
# exact four commits the author made: `git log -- godot/shaders/` is
# unchanged by this, and no agent has edited a .gdshader.
#
# If the author ever renormalises these to LF, rebuild the ledger in the same
# commit - scripts/build_lost_ledger.py re-reads every one of them.
godot/features/traps/spike_trap.gd text eol=crlf
godot/game/main.gd                 text eol=crlf
godot/shaders/dungeon_surface.gdshader text eol=crlf
godot/shaders/hint_bands.gdshader      text eol=crlf
godot/shaders/trap_warning.gdshader    text eol=crlf
# standard_generated.gdshader is NOT in that list: Godot's editor
# wrote it with LF and it is LF on disk. A `*.gdshader` glob caught it
# and the next clean clone failed on exactly that file. The list is
# per file because the split is per file, not per extension - two .gd
# files here are CRLF and the rest are LF.
godot/tests/test_trap_warning.gd   text eol=crlf

# 2026-11-08. `godot/game/main.tscn` WAS in the list above and has been
# removed, because it is no longer CRLF on disk. Assignment 6 added four
# nodes to it -- DungeonMotes, HitBurstSpawner, StepDustDriver, DebugKeys --
# through scripts that write LF, so the working copy is now 3174 bytes with
# 90 LF and no CR. The attribute still said `eol=crlf`, so a clone got 3264
# bytes, and the A6 ledger -- which hashes the bytes on disk -- failed on it:
#
#     Godot gamedev evidence FAIL: Source hash mismatch: game/main.tscn
#
# This is the case the note above anticipated from the other direction. The
# attribute was right when it was written and the file moved out from under
# it. Removing the line lets `* -text` apply, so a checkout writes the
# committed bytes verbatim and the hash holds.
#
# The remaining six were re-measured on the same day and are all still CRLF
# on disk, so their lines stand. The check is one command:
#
#     python -c "import sys; d=open(sys.argv[1],chr(114)+chr(98)).read();
#                print(len(d.splitlines()), len(d))" <path>
#
#  a file whose byte count exceeds its line count by exactly its line
#  count is CRLF; one where they differ by less is LF.
#
# and it is worth running on every file in this list before any tag, because
# nothing else in the chain can see the difference: `git status` is clean in
# the clone and `git diff` prints nothing, since git knows about the
# conversion it just performed. Only a tool that reads bytes off disk sees
# it, and in this repository that tool is the course checker.
